finding: The copied-release check does not establish independence from the build tree (scripts/native-smoke.ts:1451-1484). It leaves the original .app, dist, and consumer tree available and launches open with cwd=project. The copied and original artifacts contain the same nonce; a fresh page UUID establishes a new page evaluation, not which executable/assets served it. A copy whose shell or assets still resolve through an absolute build-tree path can therefore return the hardware marker and expected shallot://localhost/ or its own loopback origin and pass. The oracle tests paths/protocols and duplicate report rejection, not this failure. Smallest fix: make the source build/assets unavailable before opening the copy, launch from an unrelated scratch cwd, restore in finally, and assert the running main executable belongs to the copied bundle (or its verified translocation), with a negative control for a build-tree-dependent copy.
